  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through Saturday...\n\nAn upper ridge continues into Monday, with warm and dry \nconditions. A wave is forecast to move through the region \ntomorrow, bringing a cold front across south-central MT and \nnorthern WY. This front will shift winds to the northwest and \nbring widespread gusts of 20 to 40 mph. No precipitation is \nexpected with this front/wave. There will be two pushes with this \nsystem, with an initial frontal passage occurring Monday morning.\nPressure rises spread across the area into the afternoon, \nbringing an additional push of winds. Minimum relative humidity \nvalues will be low, in the teens to low 20s percent. A few models\nare showing a low chance for dry and high based convection over \nthe southeastern parts of the Beartooths in the afternoon. The\nonly concerns with any convection will be a few lightning strikes\nand gusty winds.\n\nAs a result of the very warm, dry, and windy conditions, a Red \nFlag Warning is in effect from 9 AM to 9 PM Monday for portions of\nTreasure, Rosebud, Custer, Fallon, and Carter Counties. Within \nthe warning, the main concerns for elevated to critical fire \nweather conditions exist along and north of Interstate 94 and US \nHighway 12.\n\nTuesday will see calm and warm conditions, with temperatures in \nthe 70s to low 80s and little to no chance for precipitation. \nWednesday will be the warmest day of the week as the ridge axis \nmoves over the area, high temperatures are forecast in the 80s to \nlow 90s. Winds on Wednesday may be a little breezy out of the \nsouth, with gusts of 15 to 30 mph.\n\nFor the second half of the week, ensembles continue to show \nmoderate agreement that an unsettled pattern develops. Based on \nthe pattern, southwest to westerly flow breaks down the ridge and \nbrings the chance for showers and thunderstorms. The extent of any\nprecipitation remains uncertain, though this pattern \ntraditionally favors shower and thunderstorm development. Several \nensembles are showing PWATs of 0.5 to 0.75\" and modest instability\nThursday into Saturday.\n\nLook for high temperatures in the upper 60s to low 80s Thursday \ninto next weekend. Overnight temperatures are forecast to be in \nthe 40s and 50s through the week.\n\nMatos",
